<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chōsokabe Motochika</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1539">1539</a>–<a href="/years/1599">1599</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Chosokabe Motochika</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chōsokabe Motochika (1539–1599) was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of Tosa province on the Japanese island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>. Succeeding his father Kunichika in 1560, he unified Tosa by 1575 and by 1585 controlled most of Shikoku, relying on part-time farmer-soldiers called ichiryō gusoku. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>'s invasion of 1585 reduced him to Tosa, where he carried out a land survey and issued a house code in 1597.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chosokabe-motochika.jpg" alt="Chōsokabe Motochika" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%95%B7%E5%AE%97%E6%88%91%E9%83%A8%E5%85%83%E8%A6%AA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Motochika, the 21st chief of the Chōsokabe clan, was the son and heir of Chōsokabe Kunichika and a daughter of the Saitō clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mino-province">Mino Province</a>; his childhood name was Yasaburō. He is said to have been born in Okō Castle in the Nagaoka district of Tosa. Tall, fair-skinned and quiet, he is said to have worried his father with his gentle nature, which earned him the nickname Himewako, Little Princess, until he proved a brave warrior at his first battle, near Nagahama castle in 1560.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He defeated Motoyama Shigetoki at Asakura castle in 1562 and built a power base on the Kōchi plain through alliances with local families. In 1569 he marched on the Aki clan of east Tosa with 7,000 men, defeated Aki Kunitora at the Battle of Yanagare and took Aki Castle. Outwardly loyal to the Ichijō family until then, he attacked their headquarters at Nakamura in 1573, driving Ichijō Kanesada to flee to Bungo, and finished the Ichijō at the Battle of Shimantogawa in 1575.</p><p>He then turned on Iyo, where his general Kumu Yorinobu was defeated and killed at the Battle of Mimaomote in 1579, before Motochika himself led some 30,000 men there and forced Kōno Michinao to flee. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>, once his ally, grew suspicious of his advance. In 1582 Motochika defeated the Miyoshi clan at the Battle of Nakatomigawa and Sengoku Hidehisa at Hiketa. In 1585 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> sent 113,000 men against his 40,000, and after the 26-day siege of Ichinomiya Castle he surrendered Awa, Sanuki and Iyo.</p><p>In Hideyoshi's service he joined the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a> in 1587, where his son Nobuchika was killed at the Battle of Hetsugigawa, led a fleet at the sieges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimoda">Shimoda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> in 1590, and fought in the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1592. When the Spanish ship San Felipe was wrecked in his territory in 1596, he seized its cargo, and the affair led to the crucifixion of 26 Christians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, the first lethal persecution of Christians by the state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Motochika died at his mansion in Fushimi in 1599, aged 60, and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-morichika">Chōsokabe Morichika</a>. He is remembered for his 100-Article Code of the Chōsokabe and for his efforts to found an economically strong castle town, moving his seat from Oko to Otazaka and then to Urado. Nobunaga is said to have called him the bat who refuses to fly away from its home.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chōsokabe Motochika” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chosokabe Kunichika rebuilt their power, and his son <b>Motochika</b>, who succeeded in 1560, defeated rival families and the Ichijo governors to unify Tosa by 1575.</blockquote><a href="/state/chosokabe-clan-domain">Chosokabe clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35,136&amp;year=1578" aria-label="Japan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Chōsokabe Motochika"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4029 4513h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4029 4513h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35,136&amp;year=1578">What was here in 1578? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
